    {
      "id": "vh98lwg",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Constitutional Articles Related to the Supreme Court"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Articles 124 to 147 in Part V of the Indian Constitution deal with the organization, independence, jurisdiction, powers, and procedures of the Supreme Court.",
        "The Supreme Court currently consists of 34 judges (one Chief Justice of India and 33 other judges) appointed by the President.",
        "A Supreme Court judge holds office until attaining the age of 65 years and can only be removed by a Presidential order following an address by Parliament.",
        "The Original Jurisdiction under Article 131 decides federal disputes between the Centre and one or more States, or between two or more States.",
        "The Supreme Court serves as the ultimate defender and guarantor of Fundamental Rights through its writ jurisdiction under Article 32."
      ],
      "id": "6zfzi37"
    },
